ip between the algebraic multiplicity of eigenvalues and the dimension of the eigenvariety is well understood for graphs (or matrices), as it follows directly from classical linear algebra. ... Fan et al. proved that the dimension of the projective eigenvariety ${\\mathbb{V}}_{\\rho }(H)$ is zero if H is connected [13], and determined the size $|{\\mathbb{V}}_{\\rho }(H)|$ of ${\\mathbb{V}}_{\\rho }(H)$ [12].\n\n## Advances in Applied Mathematics\n\nVolume 176, May 2026, 103052\n\n# On the second-largest modulus among the eigenvalues of a power hypergraph\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.aam.2026.103052Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nIt is well known that the algebraic multiplicity of an eigenvalue of a graph (or real symmetric matrix) is equal to the dimension of its corresponding linear eigen-subspace, also known as the geometric multiplicity. However, for hypergraphs, the relationship between these two multiplicities remains an open problem. For a graph $G=(V,E)$ and $k\\ge 3$, the k-power hypergraph {G}^{(k)}${G}^{(k)}$ is a k-uniform hypergraph obtained by adding $k-2$ new vertices to each edge of G, who always has non-real eigenvalues. In this paper, we determine the second-largest modulus Λ among the eigenvalues of {G}^{(k)}${G}^{(k)}$, which is indeed an eigenvalue of {G}^{(k)}${G}^{(k)}$. The projective eigenvariety {\\mathbb{V}}_{\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}${\\mathbb{V}}_{\\mathrm{\\Lambda }}$ associated with Λ is the set of the eigenvectors of {G}^{(k)}${G}^{(k)}$ corresponding to Λ considered in the complex projective space. The relationship between the algebraic multiplicity of eigenvalues and the dimension of the eigenvariety is well understood for graphs (or matrices), as it follows directly from classical linear algebra. However, for hypergraphs (or tensors), this relationship remains an open problem, as discussed in the Hu-Ye's conjecture [18], the Fan's conjecture [11], and the verifications of these conjectures in some cases [7], [29].\n\nFor a k-uniform hypergraph H, the spectral radius $\\rho (H)$ is an eigenvalue of H as shown by the Perron-Frobenius Theorem for tensors [1]. Fan et al. proved that the dimension of the projective eigenvariety ${\\mathbb{V}}_{\\rho }(H)$ is zero if H is connected [13], and determined the size $|{\\mathbb{V}}_{\\rho }(H)|$ of ${\\mathbb{V}}_{\\rho }(H)$ [12]. The k-power hypergraph {G}^{(k)}${G}^{(k)}$ is the k-uniform hypergraph that is obtained by adding $k-2$ new vertices to each edge of a graph G for $k\\ge 3$, which is also known as the expansion of G in extremal hypergraph theory. Chen et al. characterized all of the eigenvalues of {G}^{(k)}${G}^{(k)}$ in terms of the eigenvalues of signed subgraphs of G [3]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Rigidity, Tensegrity and Reconstruction of Polytopes under Metric Constraints (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/368877627_Rigidity_Tensegrity_and_Reconstruction_of_Polytopes_under_Metric_Constraints)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; the main conjecture and its corresponding “unique reconstruction version”: ... Doolittle [6]: ... WINTER\n\narc of length\n\n2 sin−1(1/√3) ≈70.5287◦>60◦.\n\nThe three edges therefore suﬃce to reach more than half around the circle. In other\n\nwords, the convex hull of q contains the circumcenter in its interior.\n\nA full-dimensional counterexample in R 3 can be obtained by interpreting q as\n\nembedded in R 2× {0}follows by a slight perturbation.\n\nFigure 6. A 2-dimensional embedding of the edge-graph of the 3-dimen-\n\nsional cube with the same circumradius and edge lengths as the unit cube,\n\nalso containing the origin in its convex hull.\n\nPotential ﬁxes to the “graph embedding versions” of Conjecture 1.1 and Conjec-\n\nture 1.2 are discussed in Section 5.2.\n\nWhile the general Conjecture 1.2 will stay open, we are conﬁdent that our meth-\n\nods point the right way and highlight the 